# --- patch 4: a Stop that always stops -- and patch 5: steering ---------------
# Both live in the server's chat path, so they are applied together, and like
# patch 3 they land on the next server start rather than the next reload.
#
# Patch 4 (always on) fixes a hang. Stop calls the SDK's interrupt(), which is a
# control request WRITTEN TO THE CLI'S STDIN -- and for a single-user-turn query,
# which is every text-only turn here, the SDK closes stdin at the first `result`.
# A CLI that outlives its own result therefore never answers: the promise never
# settles, the session is never removed (so every later Stop wedges on the same
# session), the abort handler never returns, and the client never receives its
# terminal complete. The UI sits on "processing" and Esc does nothing until the
# server is restarted. Give the control request two seconds, then close the
# transport instead -- which needs no cooperation from the child.
#
# Patch 5 (CLOUDCLI_STEER=1) is the other side of the same coin: because stdin
# is open while the turn runs, a user frame pushed into it is picked up at the
# next agent-loop boundary, in the SAME turn. So a message typed mid-run can
# steer the run instead of queueing behind it. Nothing about how the turn was
# started has to change, which is what makes this small.
#
# The CLI applies such a frame but never writes it down, and CloudCLI's history
# IS the CLI's transcript -- so patch 5 also appends the line there itself, in
# the shape a user turn has. Without that the message vanishes on the next
# reload, and the CLI resumes a conversation with no record of the redirection.
#
# The one trap: streamInput() calls endInput() when its iterable ENDS, closing
# stdin -- which would take interrupt(), and with it Stop, down with it. The
# queue therefore never ends on its own; it is closed from removeSession(),
# which is reached only on completion, error and abort.
#
# Patch 5 is all-or-nothing across the three files by construction: a facade
# naming a function that failed to be inserted is a ReferenceError at import,
# i.e. a server that does not start. And every edit it makes is REVERSED when
# the flag is off, so the flag is the whole switch -- turning steering off is a
# restart, not a reinstall.
RTP="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/providers/list/claude/claude-runtime.provider.js"
PRS="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/providers/services/provider-runtime.service.js"
CWS="$PKG/dist-server/server/modules/websocket/services/chat-websocket.service.js"
if [[ -f $RTP && -f $PRS && -f $CWS ]]; then
  kit_revert() { for f in "$RTP" "$PRS" "$CWS"; do mv -f "$f.prepatch" "$f"; done; }
  for f in "$RTP" "$PRS" "$CWS"; do cp "$f" "$f.prepatch"; done
  if python3 - "$RTP" "$PRS" "$CWS" "$STEER" <<'PY'
import sys

rtp, prs, cws, steer = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4] == '1'
applied, already, missing, removed = [], [], [], []


def edit(text, old, new, label, count=1):
    """Apply one exact edit. Returns the new text, or None if the anchor moved.

    Re-runnable: this script patches the installed file in place, so on every
    start after the first the anchor is already gone and the result is already
    there. That is success, not a miss -- and an upgrade, which restores the
    file, comes back through the anchor path.
    """
    if text is None:
        return None
    if new in text:
        already.append(label)
        return text
    if text.count(old) != count:
        missing.append('%s (%d anchors)' % (label, text.count(old)))
        return None
    applied.append(label)
    return text.replace(old, new, count)


def unedit(text, old, new, label):
    """Undo one edit. Cannot fail: absent means it was never applied."""
    if new not in text:
        return text
    removed.append(label)
    return text.replace(new, old)


# --- patch 4 ----------------------------------------------------------------
